00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:32.480
what's important about these behaviors&nbsp;
is that they emerge early in development&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:37.920
and there's a lot of overlap in symptomatology so&nbsp;
the main diagnosis you'll hear about with ndds are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:43.600
things like global developmental delay autism&nbsp;
spectrum disorder and intellectual disability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:48.400
there's others like adhd and social communication&nbsp;
disorder but it's really global delay intellectual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:52.480
disability and autism that you'll be&nbsp;
that you'll hear me talking about and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:56.400
you'll hear me talking about work that's really&nbsp;
focused on autism but i just want to emphasize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:00.960
that a lot of these developmental disabilities&nbsp;
overlap what's important to know though is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:05.120
there's a tremendous amount of heterogeneity&nbsp;
in these different diagnoses right so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:08.480
you know you meet one there's a famous quote that&nbsp;
if you've met one child with autism you've met&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:13.840
one child with autism um but the reality is that&nbsp;
there is a lot of variability in how kids get to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:19.120
these list of symptoms if you will um and part of&nbsp;
the reason for that heterogeneity is that there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:23.840
a lot of ideological heterogeneity now there's a&nbsp;
high genetic liability and we'll talk about that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:28.480
but there's many different biological&nbsp;
and even environmental ways that one can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:33.040
arrive at these developmental disabilities and so&nbsp;
that really is actually a challenge for us when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:39.120
we're thinking about designing treatments or even&nbsp;
you know trying to identify clinical patterns that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:45.200
we might want to target again with treatments or&nbsp;
with evaluations so despite all that heterogeneity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:50.160
though our treatment model has really historically&nbsp;
been this so i see a kiddo in clinic say with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:56.240
autism diagnosis right so autism you have core&nbsp;
um symptoms or deficits and social behavior as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.240 --> 00:08:00.800
well as the presence of repetitive behaviors and&nbsp;
restricted interests or rbs so i diagnose a child&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:06.400
with autism and then i essentially recommend a&nbsp;
treatment and that treatment might be a behavioral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:11.280
intervention or it might be a medication and then&nbsp;
basically what i'm looking for is whether those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:16.640
basic core symptoms have changed so i'm measuring&nbsp;
quite crudely often that outcome which would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:21.360
be again those core symptoms um and again the&nbsp;
treatment could be a medication or a behavioral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:27.040
intervention but you know it feels crude and as a&nbsp;
clinician who's trying to be precise in diagnosis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:31.440
it becomes a bit frustrating at times to then say&nbsp;
you know i've made this really precise diagnosis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:36.480
and now i'm just going to have you go and you know&nbsp;
access whatever services your regional center or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:42.000
your school system can provide you and we'll hope&nbsp;
that we see a change in your outcome we'd like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:46.240
to move towards what we consider precision health&nbsp;
and i'm going to sort of operationalize precision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:49.840
health in a few different ways for you guys but&nbsp;
you know the idea of precision health is that as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:56.000
we start understanding causes a bit better and we&nbsp;
identify specific clinical symptoms that may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:01.280
more proximal to the cause we then can identify&nbsp;
treatments that actually might be targeting those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:06.240
specific symptoms or even the cause and then our&nbsp;
outcomes that we're measuring might be a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:11.840
more specific again to that subgroup or to that&nbsp;
cause and ideally would be evidence-based so we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:16.800
actually have data on what those outcomes might be&nbsp;
that might be measurable um so this is kind of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:21.600
basic rubric really simplistic of where we'd like&nbsp;
to move from the standpoint of more precision help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:27.600
in neurodevelopmental disorders i love this&nbsp;
quote from um obama timely this week to sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:32.880
of quote him from his state of the union address&nbsp;
you know many years ago where he really put forth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:37.920
a plan for a large federally funded initiative&nbsp;
towards precision health that was um largely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:42.720
sponsored by nih where he defined precision&nbsp;
health as delivering the right treatment to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:47.440
the right patient every time and i think that's&nbsp;
definitely a goal in our case as well i'll add a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:51.840
few caveats so at this point the one caveat will&nbsp;
add to that in neurodevelopmental disabilities is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:56.800
that we also want to make sure that we provide the&nbsp;
right treatment to the right kid at the right time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:00.880
and timing is often we think key in these&nbsp;
developmental disabilities where we think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:05.360
that changes are occurring even in utero right&nbsp;
so we'd like to try to target treatments as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:08.960
early as possible which is challenging for&nbsp;
a variety of reasons as we'll talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:14.160
so how do we even start thinking about precision&nbsp;
health and neurodevelopmental disorders like where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:18.960
do we look given this big heterogeneous&nbsp;
behavioral set of conditions that we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:23.040
and this is where genetics has actually been&nbsp;
incredibly transformative and again i'm going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:27.600
summarize a bunch of stuff in sort of more simple&nbsp;
slides just to make the point but you know we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:33.680
had a lot of advances in how we examine clinically&nbsp;
examine you know genetic etiologies of disease and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:38.000
and conditions um you know we just use the&nbsp;
basic karyotype to look at changes in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:42.640
number of chromosomes that's moved now to&nbsp;
using microarray technology to identify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:49.520
duplications or deletions in the chromosomes that&nbsp;
are you know up to 50 or 100 kilobases in size and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:55.040
then of course now even more clinically common is&nbsp;
is actually whole exome or whole genome sequencing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.040 --> 00:11:00.080
where we can identify changes at this at the level&nbsp;
of the single base pair which helps us to identify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:06.000
single gene disorders um and with that technology&nbsp;
what's happened is that and this is just a rubric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:11.520
is that we've actually identified um you know not&nbsp;
just these big single gene disorders like tuberous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:15.440
sclerosis that we've known about for a long&nbsp;
time that cause neurodevelopmental disabilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:19.760
but also copy number variance syndromes&nbsp;
like 15q duplication syndrome that you'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:24.080
hear me talk about a bit later as well&nbsp;
as these very small single gene mutations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:28.080
and now taken together you know&nbsp;
these don't account for all of autism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:32.640
or all of neurodevelopmental disabilities&nbsp;
but the number is increasing in terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:38.160
the number of um kids or even adults that we&nbsp;
identified that have these putative causes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:44.400
and what's important actually and interesting now&nbsp;
is that with all that all the research done in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:48.880
in sort of autism and neurodevelopmental genetics&nbsp;
it's now actually translated to clinical care and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:54.080
now genetic testing is really the only routinely&nbsp;
recommended medical workup for an individual with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:59.040
autism or you know intellectual disability um&nbsp;
and the clinical gold standard is that we do a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:05.520
microarray and then some other testing based on&nbsp;
the sex of the child um whole exome sequencing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:10.080
is often performed if the microarray is negative&nbsp;
because remember the microarray identifies copy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:15.040
number variants it will miss single gene&nbsp;
um mutations and other kinds of very small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:20.880
variants and so exome is performed there's been a&nbsp;
bunch of meta-analyses that i'm not going to show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:25.440
here that have looked at the clinical utility of&nbsp;
whole exome sequencing and it's found in certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:32.640
cases where there's a high pretest probability&nbsp;
uh that the the um the yield can be as high as 50&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:37.280
so we're really moving into this place and the&nbsp;
next set of recommendations probably will include&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:41.760
whole exome sequencing so why is that important&nbsp;
well first of all we've identified now that about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:46.560
25 of individuals with a neurodevelopmental&nbsp;
disability have an identified genetic cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:50.960
so we're starting to think about you know&nbsp;
can we learn something from these syndromic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:54.960
neurodevelopmental disorders because we know cause&nbsp;
at this point right and then again if we think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.960 --> 00:13:01.280
about precision health we've opened a big door&nbsp;
um so before i sort of get into that um i want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:05.840
just make a couple of important clinical uh points&nbsp;
that i think are really relevant when we think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:11.840
about these kids with identified genetic causes&nbsp;
so one is that clinically these kids do tend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:15.040
be more affected and i want to be really clear&nbsp;
about that sometimes you know people talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:20.320
you know certain genetic conditions as&nbsp;
being models for autism or models for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:24.400
neurodevelopmental disease and i always am very&nbsp;
clear to say like you know these kids with some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:29.600
these really large de novo mutations or variants&nbsp;
they're very clinically affected so they don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:33.840
represent the whole spectrum right they often&nbsp;
represent the end of the spectrum that are more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:39.440
maybe clinically challenged in different ways&nbsp;
and what does that mean so these kids often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:43.440
don't just have an autism diagnosis and i don't&nbsp;
mean to say just to belittle the importance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:47.840
that diagnosis rather that they don't they&nbsp;
don't simply have the social communication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:54.080
challenges or repetitive behaviors they also&nbsp;
have global developmental delay especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:59.600
motor delays and hypotonia often have comorbid&nbsp;
intellectual disability and importantly often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:03.840
have other comorbidities not just medical&nbsp;
ones but neurological ones like epilepsy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:09.280
so in certain conditions we're seeing epilepsy in&nbsp;
up to 50 or 75 percent of children and epilepsy is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:15.520
just having more than one um unexplained or un um&nbsp;
unprovoked seizure uh so you know these are kids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:19.760
who are a little bit more medically and clinically&nbsp;
complex but you could argue these are also kids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:23.840
where we're really desperate for treatments right&nbsp;
to really mitigate some of these issues that occur&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:29.440
over a lifetime the other really important point&nbsp;
though and again has been a really huge source of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:34.720
advancement actually over the last many years&nbsp;
is that as these individual rare conditions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.720 --> 00:14:38.720
